/-!
# TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.James — James 2:24, and the increase of justification

> "You see that a person is justified by works and not by faith alone" (James
> 2:24).

Few verses have been argued over more in this debate. Trent read it as teaching
the increase of justification. The justified, says the decree,

> "increase in that justice which they have received ... and are still further
> justified, as it is written; He that is just, let him be justified still; and
> again, Be not afraid to be justified even to death; and also, Do you see that
> by works a man is justified, and not by faith only" (Session VI, ch. 10).

Canon 24 condemns anyone who says good works "are merely the fruits and signs
of Justification obtained, but not a cause of the increase thereof". This
chapter weighs that reading, the three texts the decree cites for it, and the
Reformed reply.

## Two ways of taking Trent's reading

Trent's claim about James 2:24 can be taken two ways, and each meets different
evidence.

- **As a claim about James's word.** "Justified" (δικαιόω) in James *means* the
  increase of the justice already received: being made more just. Then it meets
  the evidence about the word. Outside Paul, the word declares, acquits or
  vindicates: "wisdom is justified by her deeds" (Matthew 11:19, ESV), where
  "deeds" translates ἔργα, the word James uses for "works". James uses it of
  Abraham offering Isaac (Genesis 22), long after "Abraham believed God, and it
  was counted to him as righteousness" (James 2:23, quoting Genesis 15:6), and
  of Rahab. And a word contributes the least meaning its context requires.
- **As a claim about what works do.** James's "justified" is a verdict, and the
  works it is "by" are a cause of that verdict's increase, not only its fruit
  (canon 24). Nothing about the word bears on this. It meets the Reformed
  reading, on which the works are evidence of a living faith, and at that point
  each side rests on its own confession.

## The other two texts Trent cites

Two of Trent's three texts read differently in the Latin Trent quotes and in the
Greek.

"He that is just, let him be justified still" is the Latin Vulgate's wording
(*iustificetur adhuc*) at Revelation 22:11. The Greek, as modern critical
editions print it, says "let the righteous still do righteousness", and the
Catholic Church's own English translation gives "The righteous must still do
right". The printed Textus Receptus of the sixteenth century reads "let him be
justified still" (δικαιωθήτω), as the Latin does; the critical editions and the
Byzantine Majority Text do not.

"Be not afraid to be justified even to death" is the Vulgate's Sirach 18:22. The
Greek does have the verb "to be justified" (δικαιωθῆναι), but in a sentence
about vows: "let nothing hinder you from paying a vow in due time, and do not
wait until death to be justified". The Catholic Church's own translation renders
the clause "do not wait until death to fulfill them". So the Latin and the Greek
share "until death ... to be justified". They differ in the rest: the Latin has
"pray always" and "be not afraid", where the Greek has "pay a vow" and "do not
wait".

Pius XII teaches that the original text, "written by the inspired author
himself, has more authority and greater weight than any even the very best
translation" (*Divino Afflante Spiritu* §16). Westminster says the same (I.8).
So taken as claims about what the inspired authors wrote, the two Latin readings
cannot be defended.

Pius XII does not say that a Latin reading absent from the original is an error.
Of the Vulgate as a whole he says the opposite. As the Church understands it, it
is "free from any error whatsoever in matters of faith and morals", and its
authority is "juridical", that is, a matter of the Church's approval, and not a
claim about the original wording (§21). Taken that way, Trent's "as it is
written" at these two verses quotes the Church's approved Latin and not the
inspired author's words, and is consistent with everything Pius XII says
(the chapter "Trent's two Latin texts"). What it then rests on is the Church's
authority. Pius XII himself asks that a doctrine taught in this way be confirmed
from the original texts (§22), and at these two verses the originals do not
confirm it.

## The phrase "faith alone"

James 2:24 is the one place in the New Testament where the phrase occurs, and it
is often cited as denying *sola fide* in so many words. The faith James says
does not justify is the faith he calls dead (2:17, 2:26). Trent reads it so:
faith "unless hope and charity be added thereto ... For which reason it is most
truly said, that Faith without works is dead" (Session VI, ch. 7). The
Westminster Confession, a Reformed confession of 1647, says the faith that alone
justifies "is no dead faith, but worketh by love" (Westminster XI.2). So the
phrase James denies is not the formula the Reformers affirm (`jamesFaithLine`).
This answers an argument from the phrase. It does not answer James's "by works",
which is the question above.

## The words used here

These are the words of the first chapter, which "Weighing the positions" uses
too. One position
**defeats** another when it contradicts something the other rests on, concludes
or derives, and is not the weaker. A position is **forced** when it must be
accepted however every tie is settled. It **can be defended** when some
consistent set of positions includes it and answers every attack. It **cannot be
defended** when none does.

## What the debate decides

- **Forced** (`james_words_prevail`). James's "justified" does not mean the
  increase of justice. The faith James 2:24 denies is not the Reformers' faith.
  Revelation 22:11, in the critical Greek text, does not say "be justified
  still". Sirach 18:22 in Greek does not say "be not afraid to be justified even
  to death".
- **Cannot be defended.** Trent's reading of James's word
  (`trent_on_james_word_indefensible`), and its use of Revelation 22:11
  (`trent_on_revelation_indefensible`) and Sirach 18:22
  (`trent_on_sirach_indefensible`) as texts for the increase of justification.
- **Can be defended, and not forced.** Trent's reading of what works do
  (`trent_on_works_defensible`, `trent_on_works_not_forced`), and, against it,
  the Reformed reconciliation of James with Paul. Whether works are only the
  fruit of justification is contested at the ratings the sources were given,
  before they are recomputed under the three standards (`worksContested`).
  Trent's reading of the texts that promise a reward (chapter 16,
  `trentRewardLine`) meets the Reformed reconciliation in the same way. It also
  meets Westminster's reading of the same texts (`westminsterRewardLine`), which
  denies that the reward is given to merits. When only evidence anyone can check
  is counted, neither of Trent's readings of what works do stands as an
  objection, and the Reformed reconciliation is forced
  (the chapter "Why each step is rated as it is").
- **Cannot be defended, at the ratings the sources were given.** The reading on
  which James's word means renewal because Paul's does (`renewalWordLine`). The
  argument from James's word defeats it. When a tradition's own teaching is
  allowed to count as an objection, it does stand as one, and then the question
  is contested (the chapter "Why each step is rated as it is"). It also meets the
  Reformed reconciliation, and the two defeat each other: the reconciliation
  says James's word does not have Paul's sense, and this reading says it does.

So of the three texts chapter 10 cites, one is left: James 2:24, taken as a
claim about what works do before God. Each reading of the three that rests on
what a word or a verse *says* cannot be defended. Trent's reading of the reward
texts (chapter 16) can also be defended. No other text was weighed here. What
remains is a claim about God's verdict, and there these pages cannot choose.

## A rating that was lowered, against the Reformed side

The premise that works are the fruit of faith and not its ground
(`worksAreFruitNotGround`) was rated `wellSupported` while no position here
denied it. In this debate Trent's reading of what works do denies it, and the
rating decides who prevails. So the rating was re-examined. Canon 24 condemns
exactly that claim. As a premise it is now `disputed`. Left at `wellSupported`,
it would have made Trent's reading impossible to defend, and that verdict would
have come from the rating and not from the evidence.

As a step (`fruitLine`), its rating is now worked out and not simply assigned
(the chapter "Why each step is rated as it is"). Its support is `wellSupported`: the
*Joint Declaration* confesses works to be fruits (§37) and not the basis of
justification (§25). On whether works contribute anything further, it records
the sides apart: Catholics hold that good works "contribute to growth in grace"
(§38). Each of Trent's two objections to it rests, at one claim, on the
council's own word. So the step stands when only checkable evidence counts, and
is `disputed` when a tradition's teaching counts. This debate weighs the
Reformed reconciliation at its weakest link in any case: that James is attacking
a barren faith is rated `disputed` (`jamesHarmonySource`).

## What each position rests on

`james_warrants` lists, for each position, the claims it makes on someone's word
alone. Each of Trent's five readings rests on Trent's word: four at the reading
itself, and the reading of the reward texts at the claim that the reward is
given to merits. The reading through Paul rests on Trent's word at one claim,
that James uses the word as Paul does. The two readings of the Greek rest, at
"the original text outweighs any translation", on the word of Pius XII and of
Westminster. Both sides accept it, so it decides the matter between them. It is
still a claim taken on authority. The argument from James's word, the argument
about James's faith, and the Reformed reconciliation rest on nothing but
Scripture and evidence. This is reported beside the verdicts and does not affect
them.

## Westminster's texts for "fruit, not ground"

The Reformed reconciliation does not assume that works are the fruit of faith
and not its ground. It derives it (`fruitLine`) from the texts the Westminster
Assembly gave as proofs. Good works "are the fruits and evidences of a true and
lively faith" (XVI.2: James 2:18, 22; Ephesians 2:10; Romans 6:22). And "we
cannot by our best works merit" (XVI.5: Ephesians 2:8–9; Luke 17:10).

Trent accepts the texts, and the word "fruit": its chapter 16 is headed "On the
fruit of Justification, that is, on the merit of good works", and canon 24
condemns only those who call works "*merely* the fruits and signs". The
disagreement is whether the fruit is also merit. Trent's own answer, that the
reward the texts promise is given to merits, is a claim it makes on its own word
("we must believe", chapter 16).

So the Reformed reconciliation now rests on Scripture and not on the
Confession's word. In this debate, at the ratings the sources were given,
whether works are only fruit is still contested (`worksContested`). When only
checkable evidence counts, it is not (the chapter "Why each step is rated as it is").

## What has been re-examined, and what has not

The steps in the argument from James's word and the argument about James's faith
are cited at `wellSupported`.

The rating of the step about the word is now worked out as well
(the chapter "Why each step is rated as it is"). The strongest known objection to it is
the reading that takes James's word as Paul's and Paul's as renewal
(`renewalWordLine`). That reading accepts the step's starting points and argues
for the opposite conclusion. But one of its own starting points, that James uses
the word as Paul does, rests on Trent's word alone. So the step stands at
`wellSupported` when only checkable evidence counts, and is `disputed` when a
tradition's teaching counts. This debate weighs it at its cited rating, the
first of these. A scholar, verified, who holds that James's word is Paul's would
make that objection stand either way, and the step `disputed` either way.

The step about James's faith has no objection written down here beyond Howell's,
which states the opposite without arguing it
(`TestimonyArguments.Articles.Howell2003`). A fuller search across the Catholic
commentaries on James is still to be done.
-/

namespace TestimonyArguments.SolaFide

open Testimony

open Testimony Testimony.Bib Testimony.Logic Testimony.Logic.Framework Testimony.Scripture

/-! ### How each step is rated -/

/-- The step from three things to "James's 'justified' does not mean the increase of
justice": how the word (δικαιόω) is used outside Paul, how James himself uses
it, and the rule of least meaning. Its rating has yet to be re-examined. -/
def jamesLexicalSource : Source :=
  { primary := .work mooJames (.adLoc ⟨.james, 2, 21⟩)
  , supporting := [.work silvaBiblicalWords .whole, .work barrSemantics .whole]
  , tradition := .christianHistoricalGrammatical
  , confidence := .wellSupported }

/-- The step from James 2:14–26, the dead faith it names, and what the Reformers'
formula means, to "the faith James 2:24 denies is not the Reformers' faith". Its
rating has yet to be re-examined. -/
def jamesFaithSource : Source :=
  { primary := .work mooJames (.adLoc ⟨.james, 2, 24⟩)
  , supporting := [.work westminsterConfession (.sectionRef "XI.2")]
  , tradition := .christianHistoricalGrammatical
  , confidence := .wellSupported }

/-- Trent's reading of its three texts as teaching the increase of justification
(Session VI, ch. 10). Rated `disputed`: the Reformed accept the texts and deny
that they teach it. -/
def trentIncreaseSource : Source :=
  { primary := .work tannerDecrees (.sectionRef "Trent, Session VI (1547), ch. 10")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- Trent's reading of what works do (Session VI, canon 24), applied to "by works"
in James 2:24. Rated `disputed`: the Reformed accept James's words and read the
works as evidence, not cause. -/
def trentWorksSource : Source :=
  { primary := .work tannerDecrees (.sectionRef "Trent, Session VI (1547), canon 24")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- The step from what the original text says, and the principle that it outweighs
any translation, to "the inspired text does not say what the Latin says". Rated
`wellSupported`: Pius XII (*Divino Afflante Spiritu* §16) and Westminster (I.8)
both make the original the measure of a translation, and no reader was found who
accepts both and holds that the inspired author wrote what only the Latin has. -/
def textualSource : Source :=
  { primary := .work divinoAfflanteSpiritu (.sectionRef "§16")
  , supporting := [.work sblgnt .whole, .work nabre .whole]
  , tradition := .christianHistoricalGrammatical
  , confidence := .wellSupported }

/-- The step in the Reformed reconciliation: from "James attacks a barren faith" and
"works are the fruit of saving faith" to "James 2:24 does not contradict Paul".
Rated `disputed`: Trent accepts James's words and reads the works as a cause of
the increase of justification (Session VI, ch. 10, canon 24). -/
def jamesHarmonySource : Source :=
  { primary := .work mooJames (.adLoc ⟨.james, 2, 24⟩)
  , supporting := [.work calvinInstitutes (.sectionRef "III.xvii.11")]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- Trent's step from the texts that promise a reward, and the reward as given to
merits, to "works are not merely fruit". Eternal life is "a reward to be
faithfully rendered to their good works and merits" (Session VI, ch. 16), and
the good works of the justified truly merit it (canon 32). Rated `disputed`:
Westminster accepts that God rewards the good works of believers, and holds the
reward to be of grace, given to works accepted in Christ and not earned by them
(XVI.5, XVI.6). -/
def trentRewardSource : Source :=
  { primary := .work tannerDecrees (.sectionRef "Trent, Session VI (1547), ch. 16, canon 32")
  , supporting := [.work westminsterConfession (.sectionRef "XVI.5, XVI.6")]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- The step in the reading that takes James's word through Paul's: from Paul's
"justify" as renewal, James's word as Paul's, and James's "justified by works"
coming after Genesis 15:6, to "James's 'justified' means the increase of
justice" (Trent, Session VI, ch. 10). Rated `disputed`: the argument from
James's word accepts James's words and denies the conclusion. -/
def renewalWordSource : Source :=
  { primary := .work tannerDecrees (.sectionRef "Trent, Session VI (1547), ch. 10")
  , supporting := [.work vanLandinghamJudgment .whole]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- Westminster's step from the texts it gives as proofs to "the reward is not given
to merits". God does not forget the work of believers (Hebrews 6:10, a proof of
XVI.6, and one of Trent's own four texts). When they have done all, they are
unworthy servants who have done only their duty (Luke 17:10, XVI.5). To one who
works, wages are counted as due, and to one who believes, as a gift (Romans
4:4–5, XVI.5). And their sacrifices are acceptable to God through Jesus Christ
(1 Peter 2:5, XVI.6). So God "is pleased to accept and reward" works accepted in
his Son, and the reward is of grace, not merit (XVI.5, XVI.6). Rated `disputed`:
Trent accepts these texts and holds the reward to be given to merits (Session
VI, ch. 16, canon 32). -/
def westminsterRewardSource : Source :=
  { primary := .work westminsterConfession (.sectionRef "XVI.5, XVI.6")
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-! ### The lines of argument -/

/-- **James's word**, read by the rule of least meaning. Outside Paul, "justify"
(δικαιόω) declares, acquits or vindicates. James uses it of Abraham at Genesis
22, after Genesis 15:6 had been spoken, and of Rahab. And a word contributes the
least meaning its context requires. So James's "justified" does not mean the
increase of justice. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def jamesLexicalLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "James's word (δικαιόω, by the rule of least meaning)"
  , grounds := [p .dikaioIsDeclarativeOutsidePaul, p .james2JustifiedByWorks, p .leastMeaning]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .dikaioIsDeclarativeOutsidePaul, p .james2JustifiedByWorks, p .leastMeaning]
      ➝ notP .jamesJustifyDenotesIncrease
  , delivers := notP .jamesJustifyDenotesIncrease
  , inference := some jamesLexicalSource }

/-- **James's faith.** The faith that James 2:24 says does not justify is the faith
apart from works that he calls dead. The faith the Reformers say alone justifies
is no dead faith. So the phrase James denies is not the formula they affirm. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def jamesFaithLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "James's faith (the faith James calls dead)"
  , grounds :=
      [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .jamesFaithAloneIsDeadFaith,
        p .reformedFaithIsNoDeadFaith]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .jamesFaithAloneIsDeadFaith,
          p .reformedFaithIsNoDeadFaith]
      ➝ p .jamesFaithIsNotReformedFaith
  , delivers := p .jamesFaithIsNotReformedFaith
  , inference := some jamesFaithSource }

/-- **Trent's reading of James's word**: "justified by works" is the increase of the
justice received, so James 2:24 teaches the increase of justification. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def catholicJamesWordLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "James 2:24, read for the increase of justification"
  , grounds :=
      [p .james2_24NotByFaithAlone, p .james2JustifiedByWorks, p .jamesJustifyDenotesIncrease]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .james2_24NotByFaithAlone, p .james2JustifiedByWorks,
          p .jamesJustifyDenotesIncrease]
      ➝ p .scriptureTeachesIncreaseOfJustification
  , delivers := p .scriptureTeachesIncreaseOfJustification
  , inference := some trentIncreaseSource }

/-- **Trent's reading of what works do**, applied to James 2:24: the works by which
a person is justified are a cause of the increase, so they are not merely fruit
and evidence. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def catholicJamesWorksLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "James 2:24, read by canon 24"
  , grounds :=
      [p .james2_24NotByFaithAlone, p .james2JustifiedByWorks,
        p .worksCauseIncreaseOfJustification]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .james2_24NotByFaithAlone, p .james2JustifiedByWorks,
          p .worksCauseIncreaseOfJustification]
      ➝ notP .worksAreFruitNotGround
  , delivers := notP .worksAreFruitNotGround
  , inference := some trentWorksSource }

/-- **Trent's Revelation 22:11**: "He that is just, let him be justified still". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def trentRevelationLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Revelation 22:11, as Trent quotes it"
  , grounds := [p .rev22_11BeJustifiedStill]
  , step := p .rev22_11BeJustifiedStill ➝ p .scriptureTeachesIncreaseOfJustification
  , delivers := p .scriptureTeachesIncreaseOfJustification
  , inference := some trentIncreaseSource }

/-- **Trent's Sirach 18:22**: "Be not afraid to be justified even to death". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def trentSirachLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Sirach 18:22, as Trent quotes it"
  , grounds := [p .sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath]
  , step := p .sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath ➝ p .scriptureTeachesIncreaseOfJustification
  , delivers := p .scriptureTeachesIncreaseOfJustification
  , inference := some trentIncreaseSource }

/-- **Revelation 22:11 in Greek.** The verse says "let the righteous still do
righteousness". The original outweighs any translation. So its inspired text
does not say "be justified still". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def revelationTextLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Revelation 22:11, in the Greek"
  , grounds := [p .rev22_11GreekDoRighteousness, p .originalTextDecides]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .rev22_11GreekDoRighteousness, p .originalTextDecides] ➝ notP .rev22_11BeJustifiedStill
  , delivers := notP .rev22_11BeJustifiedStill
  , inference := some textualSource }

/-- **Sirach 18:22 in Greek.** The verse is about paying a vow promptly, and says
"do not wait until death to be justified". The original outweighs any
translation. So its inspired text does not say what the Latin says, "be not
afraid to be justified even to death". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def sirachTextLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Sirach 18:22, in the Greek"
  , grounds := [p .sir18_22GreekIsAVow, p .originalTextDecides]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .sir18_22GreekIsAVow, p .originalTextDecides] ➝ notP .sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath
  , delivers := notP .sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath
  , inference := some textualSource }

/-- **The reward of works, as Trent reads it.** God rewards the labour of believers
(1 Corinthians 15:58, Hebrews 6:10, Hebrews 10:35, 2 Timothy 4:8). The reward is
given to those works as merits (chapter 16, canon 32). So works are not merely
the fruit of justification. The second premise is stated as Trent states it. The
texts say "reward"; that the reward is given to merits is the council's own
claim, introduced with "we must believe". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def trentRewardLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The reward of works (Trent, Session VI, ch. 16)"
  , grounds := [p .rewardTextsPromiseReward, p .rewardRenderedToMerits]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .rewardTextsPromiseReward, p .rewardRenderedToMerits]
      ➝ notP .worksAreFruitNotGround
  , delivers := notP .worksAreFruitNotGround
  , inference := some trentRewardSource }

/-- **James's word, read through Paul's.** Paul's "justify" (δικαιόω) means the
renewal of the inward person, as Augustine explained it and as VanLandingham
argues from the lexicon. James uses the word as Paul does. And James says
Abraham was justified by works at Genesis 22, after Genesis 15:6 had been
spoken. So James's "justified" means the increase of the justice received.

This is the strongest reading of the word against the argument from James's
usage that has been found: it argues for the increase from Paul's word, and does
not merely state it. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def renewalWordLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "James's word, read through Paul's (the renewal reading)"
  , grounds :=
      [p .paulsJustifyDenotesRenewal, p .jamesUsesDikaioAsPaul, p .james2JustifiedByWorks]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .paulsJustifyDenotesRenewal, p .jamesUsesDikaioAsPaul, p .james2JustifiedByWorks]
      ➝ p .jamesJustifyDenotesIncrease
  , delivers := p .jamesJustifyDenotesIncrease
  , inference := some renewalWordSource }

/-- **The reward of works, as Westminster reads it** (XVI.5, XVI.6). God rewards the
labour of believers, Hebrews 6:10 among the texts. Having done all, they are
unworthy servants who have done only their duty (Luke 17:10). Wages are counted
as due to one who works, and as a gift to one who believes (Romans 4:4–5). And
their sacrifices are acceptable to God through Jesus Christ (1 Peter 2:5). So
the reward is not given to their works as merits. Every premise is a text, and
Westminster gives each as a proof of these sections. What is Westminster's own
is the step, a reading of the texts, rated `disputed`. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def westminsterRewardLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The reward of works, of grace (Westminster XVI.5, XVI.6)"
  , grounds :=
      [ p .rewardTextsPromiseReward, p .luke17_10UnworthyServants, p .romans4_4_5
      , p .firstPeter2_5AcceptableThroughChrist ]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [ p .rewardTextsPromiseReward, p .luke17_10UnworthyServants, p .romans4_4_5
        , p .firstPeter2_5AcceptableThroughChrist ]
      ➝ notP .rewardRenderedToMerits
  , delivers := notP .rewardRenderedToMerits
  , inference := some westminsterRewardSource }

/-! ### The positions -/

/-- James's word: "justified" in James does not mean the increase of justice. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def jamesLexicalCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  jamesLexicalLine.asPackage baseCite "James's δικαιόω does not denote the increase of justice"

/-- James's faith: the phrase James denies is not the Reformers' formula. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def jamesFaithCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  jamesFaithLine.asPackage baseCite "The faith James 2:24 denies is not the Reformers' faith"

/-- Westminster's reading of its proof texts: from Ephesians 2:8–10, James 2:18–22,
Romans 6:22 and Luke 17:10 to "works are the fruit and evidence of saving faith,
not its ground" (XVI.2, XVI.5).

This rates how well the step is **supported**, and it is `wellSupported`. The
Reformed confess it. The Lutheran World Federation and the Catholic Church
confess two parts of it together: good works "follow justification and are its
fruits" (§37), and "whatever in the justified precedes or follows the free gift
of faith is neither the basis of justification nor merits it" (§25, *Joint
Declaration*).

They do not confess that works are *only* fruit. The Declaration records the
Catholic understanding that good works "contribute to growth in grace, so that
the righteousness that comes from God is preserved and communion with Christ is
deepened" (§38). That is the point canon 24 makes, and it is weighed as Trent's
objection, not as part of this step's support.

Whether an objection brings the step down is worked out separately
(the chapter "Why each step is rated as it is"). Trent objects twice: in canon 24, and
in chapter 16's reading of the reward texts. When a tradition's teaching counts,
both stand, and the step is `disputed`. When only checkable evidence counts,
neither does, because each rests at one claim on the council's word. -/
def fruitSource : Source :=
  { primary := .work westminsterConfession (.sectionRef "XVI.2, XVI.5")
  , supporting := [.work jointDeclarationJustification (.sectionRef "§§25, 37; see §38")]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .wellSupported }

/-- **Works as fruit, from Westminster's proof texts.** "Not a result of works", and
"created in Christ Jesus for good works" (Ephesians 2:8–10). Faith shown by
works (James 2:18, 22). "The fruit you get leads to sanctification" (Romans
6:22). "We are unworthy servants; we have only done what was our duty" (Luke
17:10). So works are the fruit and evidence of saving faith, not its ground. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def fruitLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Works as fruit (Westminster XVI.2, XVI.5, and its proofs)"
  , grounds :=
      [ p .ephesians2_8_9, p .ephesians2_10CreatedForGoodWorks, p .james2_18ShowFaithByWorks
      , p .romans6_22FruitToSanctification, p .luke17_10UnworthyServants ]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [ p .ephesians2_8_9, p .ephesians2_10CreatedForGoodWorks, p .james2_18ShowFaithByWorks
        , p .romans6_22FruitToSanctification, p .luke17_10UnworthyServants ]
      ➝ p .worksAreFruitNotGround
  , delivers := p .worksAreFruitNotGround
  , inference := some fruitSource }

/-- The Reformed reconciliation of James with Paul: James is attacking a barren
faith, and works are the fruit of saving faith and not its ground. The second
point is derived from Westminster's proof texts, not assumed. The line here is
`jamesLine` with "fruit, not ground" taken from `fruitLine`. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def jamesHarmonyCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { jamesLine.asPackage baseCite "James 2:24 is compatible with Paul" with
    premises := caseOf [fruitLine, jamesLine.onGrounds [p .james2TargetsDeadFaith]] [] []
    inferences := [fruitSource, jamesHarmonySource] }

/-- Trent on James's word: James 2:24 teaches the increase of justification, because
that is what James's "justified" means. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def catholicJamesOnTheWord : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  catholicJamesWordLine.asPackage baseCite "Scripture teaches the increase of justification"

/-- Trent on what works do: the works of James 2:24 are a cause of the increase of
justification, not merely its fruit. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def catholicJamesOnWorks : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  catholicJamesWorksLine.asPackage baseCite "Works are not merely the fruit of justification"

/-- Trent on Revelation 22:11. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def trentOnRevelation : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  trentRevelationLine.asPackage baseCite "Scripture teaches the increase of justification"

/-- Trent on Sirach 18:22. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def trentOnSirach : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  trentSirachLine.asPackage baseCite "Scripture teaches the increase of justification"

/-- What Revelation 22:11 says, in the Greek. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def revelationTextCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  revelationTextLine.asPackage baseCite "Revelation 22:11 does not say 'be justified still'"

/-- What Sirach 18:22 says, in the Greek. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def sirachTextCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  sirachTextLine.asPackage baseCite
    "Sirach 18:22 does not say: be not afraid to be justified even to death"

/-- Trent on the reward of works: what God rewards is not merely fruit. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def trentOnReward : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  trentRewardLine.asPackage baseCite "Works are not merely the fruit of justification"

/-- Westminster on the reward of works: the reward is of grace, and is not given to
merits. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def westminsterOnReward : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  westminsterRewardLine.asPackage baseCite "The reward is not rendered to merits"

/-- The reading through Paul: James's "justified" means the increase of justice,
because Paul's word means renewal and James's is Paul's. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def renewalOnTheWord : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  renewalWordLine.asPackage baseCite "James's δικαιόω denotes the increase of justice"

/-! ### Each position holds together and reaches its conclusion -/

/-- **The Reformed reading of James.** Every text as it stands. James's "justified"
a declaration and not an increase. The faith James denies not the Reformers'
faith. Trent's Latin readings of Revelation 22:11 and Sirach 18:22 not what the
verses say. And the reward of works not given to them as merits. -/
def reformedJamesReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .jamesJustifyDenotesIncrease => False
  | .worksCauseIncreaseOfJustification => False
  | .rev22_11BeJustifiedStill => False
  | .sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath => False
  | .scriptureTeachesIncreaseOfJustification => False
  | .rewardRenderedToMerits => False
  | _ => True

/-- **Trent's reading of James.** Every text as Trent reads it: James's "justified"
the increase of justice, its works a cause of that increase and not merely
fruit, and Revelation 22:11 and Sirach 18:22 as the Latin has them. -/
def tridentineJamesReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .worksAreFruitNotGround => False
  | _ => True

/-- The argument from James's word reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem jamesLexicalCase_establishes : Establishes jamesLexicalCase := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- The argument from James's word can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem jamesLexicalCase_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable jamesLexicalCase.premises := by
  satisfied_by reformedJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- The argument about James's faith reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem jamesFaithCase_establishes : Establishes jamesFaithCase := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- The argument about James's faith can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem jamesFaithCase_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable jamesFaithCase.premises := by
  satisfied_by reformedJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- The Reformed reconciliation reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem jamesHarmonyCase_establishes : Establishes jamesHarmonyCase := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- The Reformed reconciliation can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem jamesHarmonyCase_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable jamesHarmonyCase.premises := by
  satisfied_by reformedJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on James's word reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem catholicJamesOnTheWord_establishes : Establishes catholicJamesOnTheWord := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on James's word can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem catholicJamesOnTheWord_is_satisfiable :
    Satisfiable catholicJamesOnTheWord.premises := by
  satisfied_by tridentineJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on what works do reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem catholicJamesOnWorks_establishes : Establishes catholicJamesOnWorks := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on what works do can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem catholicJamesOnWorks_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable catholicJamesOnWorks.premises := by
  satisfied_by tridentineJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on Revelation 22:11 reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem trentOnRevelation_establishes : Establishes trentOnRevelation := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on Revelation 22:11 can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem trentOnRevelation_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable trentOnRevelation.premises := by
  satisfied_by tridentineJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on Sirach 18:22 reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem trentOnSirach_establishes : Establishes trentOnSirach := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on Sirach 18:22 can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem trentOnSirach_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable trentOnSirach.premises := by
  satisfied_by tridentineJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- The Greek of Revelation 22:11 reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem revelationTextCase_establishes : Establishes revelationTextCase := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- The Greek of Revelation 22:11 can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem revelationTextCase_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable revelationTextCase.premises := by
  satisfied_by reformedJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- The Greek of Sirach 18:22 reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem sirachTextCase_establishes : Establishes sirachTextCase := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- The Greek of Sirach 18:22 can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem sirachTextCase_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable sirachTextCase.premises := by
  satisfied_by reformedJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on the reward of works reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem trentOnReward_establishes : Establishes trentOnReward := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- Trent on the reward of works can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem trentOnReward_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable trentOnReward.premises := by
  satisfied_by tridentineJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- Westminster on the reward of works reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem westminsterOnReward_establishes : Establishes westminsterOnReward := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- Westminster on the reward of works can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem westminsterOnReward_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable westminsterOnReward.premises := by
  satisfied_by reformedJamesReading [solaFideDefs]
/-- The reading through Paul reaches its conclusion. -/
theorem renewalOnTheWord_establishes : Establishes renewalOnTheWord := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]
/-- The reading through Paul can be held without contradiction. -/
theorem renewalOnTheWord_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable renewalOnTheWord.premises := by
  satisfied_by tridentineJamesReading [solaFideDefs]

/-! ### How strong each position is -/

/-- The argument from James's word rests on nothing rated below `wellSupported`. -/
theorem jamesLexicalCase_strength : jamesLexicalCase.strength = 2 := by decide
/-- The argument about James's faith rests on nothing rated below `wellSupported`. -/
theorem jamesFaithCase_strength : jamesFaithCase.strength = 2 := by decide
/-- The Reformed reconciliation rests on its reading of what James is attacking, and
on works as fruit and not ground. Both are `disputed`. -/
theorem jamesHarmonyCase_strength : jamesHarmonyCase.strength = 0 := by decide
/-- Trent on James's word rests on its reading of the word, rated `disputed`. -/
theorem catholicJamesOnTheWord_strength : catholicJamesOnTheWord.strength = 0 := by decide
/-- Trent on what works do rests on canon 24, rated `disputed`. -/
theorem catholicJamesOnWorks_strength : catholicJamesOnWorks.strength = 0 := by decide
/-- Trent on Revelation 22:11 rests on the Latin reading, rated `disputed`. -/
theorem trentOnRevelation_strength : trentOnRevelation.strength = 0 := by decide
/-- Trent on Sirach 18:22 rests on the Latin reading, rated `disputed`. -/
theorem trentOnSirach_strength : trentOnSirach.strength = 0 := by decide
/-- The Greek of Revelation 22:11 rests on nothing below `wellSupported`. -/
theorem revelationTextCase_strength : revelationTextCase.strength = 2 := by decide
/-- The Greek of Sirach 18:22 rests on nothing below `wellSupported`. -/
theorem sirachTextCase_strength : sirachTextCase.strength = 2 := by decide
/-- Trent on the reward of works rests on its reading of the texts, rated
`disputed`. -/
theorem trentOnReward_strength : trentOnReward.strength = 0 := by decide
/-- Westminster on the reward rests on its reading of the texts, rated `disputed`. -/
theorem westminsterOnReward_strength : westminsterOnReward.strength = 0 := by decide
/-- The reading through Paul rests on Paul's word as renewal, rated `disputed`. -/
theorem renewalOnTheWord_strength : renewalOnTheWord.strength = 0 := by decide

/-! ### The debate -/

/-- The positions heard in the debate over James 2:24 and the increase of
justification. -/
inductive JamesParty
  /-- James's word: "justified" in James does not mean the increase of justice. -/
  | lexical
  /-- James's faith: the faith James 2:24 denies is not the Reformers' faith. -/
  | faith
  /-- The Reformed harmony: works the fruit of saving faith, not its ground. -/
  | harmony
  /-- Trent on James's word: "justified" is the increase of justice. -/
  | trentOnTheWord
  /-- Trent on what works do: a cause of the increase, not merely fruit. -/
  | trentOnWorks
  /-- Trent on Revelation 22:11, as the Latin has it. -/
  | trentOnRevelation
  /-- Trent on Sirach 18:22, as the Latin has it. -/
  | trentOnSirach
  /-- Revelation 22:11, in the Greek. -/
  | revelationText
  /-- Sirach 18:22, in the Greek. -/
  | sirachText
  /-- Trent on the reward of works: what God rewards is not merely fruit. -/
  | trentOnReward
  /-- The renewal reading: James's word is Paul's, and Paul's is renewal. -/
  | renewalOnTheWord
  /-- Westminster on the reward of works: of grace, not rendered to merits. -/
  | westminsterOnReward
deriving DecidableEq

/-- The premises each position argues from. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def jamesPartyNode : JamesParty → ArgumentPackage Claim
  | .lexical => jamesLexicalCase
  | .faith => jamesFaithCase
  | .harmony => jamesHarmonyCase
  | .trentOnTheWord => catholicJamesOnTheWord
  | .trentOnWorks => catholicJamesOnWorks
  | .trentOnRevelation => trentOnRevelation
  | .trentOnSirach => trentOnSirach
  | .revelationText => revelationTextCase
  | .sirachText => sirachTextCase
  | .trentOnReward => trentOnReward
  | .renewalOnTheWord => renewalOnTheWord
  | .westminsterOnReward => westminsterOnReward

/-- The debate over James 2:24 and the increase of justification. For every position
heard: its premises can be held without contradiction, its conclusion follows
from them, and every step it uses is rated.
Each position's weakest link is `wellSupported` for the readings of the words and
the texts, and the bottom for the rest. -/
declare_dispute jamesDispute := jamesPartyNode using solaFideDefs
  /-- **Who defeats whom**, for all 144 pairs.

  The argument from James's word defeats Trent's reading of that word, and the
  reply fails. It defeats the reading through Paul too, whose reply fails for the
  same reason: it rests on Paul's word as renewal, rated `disputed`, against an
  argument rated `wellSupported`.

  The Greek of Revelation 22:11 and of Sirach 18:22 defeat Trent's Latin readings
  of them, and the replies fail.

  The Reformed reconciliation and each of Trent's readings of what works do defeat
  each other: canon 24's, and chapter 16's from the reward texts. Westminster's
  reading of the reward texts and chapter 16's defeat each other: each denies what
  the other holds about merit, and both rest on a reading rated `disputed`.

  The Reformed reconciliation and the reading through Paul defeat each other
  through what their claims mean. The reconciliation holds that James 2:24 does
  not contradict Paul, which says James's word does not have Paul's sense; the
  other holds that it does (`TestimonyArguments.Meanings.SolaFide.joins`). Both
  rest on a reading rated `disputed`.

  Nothing else. In particular, nothing here contradicts the argument about James's
  faith. And the argument from James's word does not bear on Trent's reading of
  what works do, which accepts the word and makes a claim about God's verdict.

  Every answer is worked out from the positions' premises and checked by machine. -/
  defeats
    [ (.lexical, .trentOnTheWord), (.harmony, .trentOnWorks), (.trentOnWorks, .harmony)
    , (.revelationText, .trentOnRevelation), (.sirachText, .trentOnSirach)
    , (.harmony, .trentOnReward), (.trentOnReward, .harmony), (.lexical, .renewalOnTheWord)
    , (.westminsterOnReward, .trentOnReward), (.trentOnReward, .westminsterOnReward)
    , (.harmony, .renewalOnTheWord), (.renewalOnTheWord, .harmony) ]

/-! ### What the debate decides -/

/-- How far the debate can be settled. Nothing defeats the argument from James's
word, the argument about James's faith, or the Greek of the two verses, so those
are accepted first, and nothing joins them. Trent's readings of James's word, of
Revelation 22:11 and of Sirach 18:22 each have an attacker that remains: the one
that defeats it. The Reformed reconciliation and Trent's reading of what works
do have each other. -/
declare_verdict jamesWordsUnanswered for jamesDispute :
    .groundedExactly [[.lexical, .faith, .revelationText, .sirachText]]
      [ (.trentOnTheWord, .lexical), (.trentOnRevelation, .revelationText)
      , (.trentOnSirach, .sirachText), (.harmony, .trentOnWorks), (.trentOnWorks, .harmony)
      , (.trentOnReward, .harmony), (.renewalOnTheWord, .lexical)
      , (.westminsterOnReward, .trentOnReward) ]

/-- **What James's words say, and what the Greek of Trent's other two texts says,
prevails outright.** Exactly four positions are forced. James's "justified"
(δικαιόω) does not mean the increase of justice. The faith James 2:24 denies is
not the faith of the Reformers' formula. Revelation 22:11, in the critical Greek
text, does not say "be justified still". And Sirach 18:22, in Greek, does not
say "be not afraid to be justified even to death". Nothing in the debate answers
any of them.

It does not show that James 2:24 agrees with Paul, or that works are
not a cause of the increase of justification. Neither is forced. Nor that the
Greek of Sirach 18:22 lacks the word "justified": it has it, in a sentence about
vows. -/
@[headline]
theorem james_words_prevail :
    grounded jamesDispute.defeats =
      {.lexical, .faith, .revelationText, .sirachText} :=
  Eq.trans jamesWordsUnanswered.holds (by ext x; cases x <;> simp [Solver.toSet])

#print axioms james_words_prevail

/-- Why Trent's reading of James's word cannot be defended: the argument from
James's word defeats it, and nothing defeats that argument. -/
declare_verdict trentOnTheWordAnswered for jamesDispute :
    .indefensible .trentOnTheWord [(.trentOnTheWord, .lexical)]
  /-- **Trent's reading of James's word cannot be defended.** If "justified by works"
  in James 2:24 is taken to mean the increase of the justice received, being made
  more just, it cannot be defended in this debate. Outside Paul the word declares,
  acquits or vindicates. James uses it of Abraham at Genesis 22, after Genesis
  15:6 had been spoken, and of Rahab. And nothing in James's setting requires a
  second, inward sense. The step from that evidence to "the word does not mean the
  increase of justice" is rated `wellSupported`. The reading rests on Trent's word
  alone.

  It does not show that works are no cause of the increase of
  justification. That is a claim about God's verdict, not about James's word, and
  it is weighed separately (`trent_on_works_defensible`). -/
  proves @[headline] trent_on_james_word_indefensible

#print axioms trent_on_james_word_indefensible

/-- Why Trent's Revelation 22:11 cannot be defended: the Greek defeats it, and
nothing defeats the Greek. -/
declare_verdict trentOnRevelationAnswered for jamesDispute :
    .indefensible .trentOnRevelation [(.trentOnRevelation, .revelationText)]
  /-- **Trent's Revelation 22:11 cannot be defended as a text for the increase of
  justification.** "He that is just, let him be justified still" is the Latin
  Vulgate's wording (*iustificetur adhuc*). The Greek, as modern editions print it
  and as the Catholic Church's own translation gives it, says "the righteous must
  still do right". And the original, "written by the inspired author himself",
  outweighs any translation (*Divino Afflante Spiritu* §16).

  It does not show that Trent's citation is an error of doctrine. Taken
  as Pius XII takes Trent's decree on the Vulgate, as a reading the Church
  approves and holds free from error in faith, it is consistent with all he says,
  and rests on the Church's authority and not on the inspired text
  (the chapter "Trent's two Latin texts"). -/
  proves @[headline] trent_on_revelation_indefensible

#print axioms trent_on_revelation_indefensible

/-- Why Trent's Sirach 18:22 cannot be defended: the Greek defeats it, and nothing
defeats the Greek. -/
declare_verdict trentOnSirachAnswered for jamesDispute :
    .indefensible .trentOnSirach [(.trentOnSirach, .sirachText)]
  /-- **Trent's Sirach 18:22 cannot be defended as what the verse says.** "Be not
  afraid to be justified even to death" is the Vulgate's verse. The Greek, in a
  sentence about paying vows, says "do not wait until death to be justified", and
  the Catholic Church's own translation renders that "do not wait until death to
  fulfill them". Whether Sirach is Scripture is not in question here: the verdict
  holds on Trent's own list of the biblical books.

  It does not show that the Greek has no "be justified". It has the verb.
  What it lacks is the Latin's "be not afraid ... even to death", the words in
  which Trent reads continued growth in justice. -/
  proves @[headline] trent_on_sirach_indefensible

#print axioms trent_on_sirach_indefensible

/-- Why Trent's reading of what works do can be defended: it answers the one
position that attacks it, the Reformed reconciliation. -/
declare_verdict trentOnWorksStands for jamesDispute :
    .credulous .trentOnWorks [.lexical, .faith, .trentOnWorks, .revelationText, .sirachText]
  /-- **Trent's reading of what works do can be defended**, together with the argument
  from James's word, the argument about James's faith, and the Greek of both
  verses. It accepts that James's "justified" is a verdict, and holds that the
  works by which James says a person is justified are a cause of that verdict's
  increase and not merely its fruit. The Reformed reconciliation denies this, and
  the debate cannot choose: at that point each side rests on its own confession,
  rated `disputed`.

  It does not show that works are a cause of the increase. The reading is
  not forced (`trent_on_works_not_forced`). -/
  proves @[headline] trent_on_works_defensible

#print axioms trent_on_works_defensible

/-- Why it is not forced: a set of positions that can be defended includes the
Reformed reconciliation, which defeats it. -/
declare_verdict trentOnWorksAnswered for jamesDispute :
    .notSkeptical .trentOnWorks .harmony [.lexical, .faith, .harmony, .revelationText, .sirachText]
  /-- **Nor is it forced.** A consistent set of positions that answers every attack
  holds the Reformed reconciliation, with the arguments from James's word and
  James's faith, and cannot hold Trent's reading of what works do with it. -/
  proves @[headline] trent_on_works_not_forced

#print axioms trent_on_works_not_forced

/-- Why the Reformed reconciliation can be defended: it answers Trent's reading of
what works do. -/
declare_verdict harmonyStands for jamesDispute :
    .credulous .harmony [.lexical, .faith, .harmony, .revelationText, .sirachText]

/-- The Reformed reconciliation derives, from Westminster's proof texts, that works
are the fruit of faith and not its ground. -/
theorem harmony_holds_fruit :
    Entails (jamesDispute.node .harmony).premises (p .worksAreFruitNotGround) := by
  establish [solaFideDefs, jamesDispute]

/-- Trent's reading of what works do denies that. -/
theorem trentOnWorks_denies_fruit :
    Entails (jamesDispute.node .trentOnWorks).premises (∼ p .worksAreFruitNotGround) := by
  establish [solaFideDefs, jamesDispute]

/-- **Whether works are only the fruit of justification is contested, and the
weighing confirms it.** The Reformed reconciliation holds it. Trent's reading of
James 2:24 by canon 24 denies it. Each can be defended, and neither is forced.
So the rating `disputed` on that premise (`worksAreFruitNotGround`) agrees with
what the weighing finds. -/
def worksContested : Contested jamesDispute (p .worksAreFruitNotGround) where
  holder := .harmony
  denier := .trentOnWorks
  holds := harmony_holds_fruit
  denies := trentOnWorks_denies_fruit
  holderDefensible := harmonyStands.holds
  denierDefensible := trent_on_works_defensible

/-! ### What each position rests on -/

/-- **Where each position rests on someone's word alone.**

Trent's reading of the reward texts rests on the council's word at one claim:
that the reward is given to merits. The reading through Paul rests on Trent's
word at one claim: that James's "justified" is Paul's. For Paul's word as
renewal it has VanLandingham's argument from the lexicon.

Each of Trent's other four readings rests on Trent's word at the reading itself.
The two readings of the Greek rest on the word of Pius XII and of Westminster
alike, at the principle that the original outweighs any translation.

The argument from James's word, the argument about James's faith, and the
Reformed reconciliation, which now derives "fruit, not ground" from
Westminster's proof texts, rest on nothing but Scripture and evidence. -/
theorem james_warrants :
    jamesLexicalCase.restingOnAuthority = [] ∧
    jamesFaithCase.restingOnAuthority = [] ∧
    jamesHarmonyCase.restingOnAuthority = [] ∧
    catholicJamesOnTheWord.restingOnAuthority = [.jamesJustifyDenotesIncrease] ∧
    catholicJamesOnWorks.restingOnAuthority = [.worksCauseIncreaseOfJustification] ∧
    trentOnRevelation.restingOnAuthority = [.rev22_11BeJustifiedStill] ∧
    trentOnSirach.restingOnAuthority = [.sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath] ∧
    revelationTextCase.restingOnAuthority = [.originalTextDecides] ∧
    sirachTextCase.restingOnAuthority = [.originalTextDecides] ∧
    trentOnReward.restingOnAuthority = [.rewardRenderedToMerits] ∧
    renewalOnTheWord.restingOnAuthority = [.jamesUsesDikaioAsPaul] ∧
    westminsterOnReward.restingOnAuthority = [] := by
  decide

end TestimonyArguments.SolaFide
